    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Blues combines repeating harmonic form, a distinctive scale, and microtonal inflection — foundation of jazz, rock, and R&B."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "twelve-bar-blues",
      "title": "Twelve-bar blues",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "I–IV–V (often dominant sevenths) over 12 bars with standard turnarounds. Variants: 8-bar, 16-bar regional styles."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "blues-scale",
      "title": "Blues scale",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Minor pentatonic plus lowered 3rd, 5th, and 7th — 'blue notes' bent between piano keys. Vocal cry parallel to sean-nós inflection."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "folk-intersection",
      "title": "Folk intersection",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Appalachian ballads meet African American song forms. Trad bands add shuffle to hornpipes. Three-chord map for pub jams."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
